<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernardino de Sahagún</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1499–<a href="/years/1590">1590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Fray Bernardino de Sahagún</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernardino de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> was a Spanish Franciscan friar who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> in the sixteenth century. With indigenous informants and assistants he compiled the General History of the Things of New Spain, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and Spanish. It is one of the primary sources on the religion, society and culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun.jpg" alt="Bernardino de Sahagún" width="232" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardino_de_Sahag%C3%BAn_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bernardino was born Bernardino de Rivera in 1499 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a>, then strongly influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> and a centre of Franciscan intellectual life, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>. He took the name of his birth town, as Franciscan custom had it, and was probably ordained around 1527. Because of his academic and religious reputation he was recruited in 1529 to join the missionary effort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, where he spent the next 61 years. He began to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> on the voyage, learning from indigenous nobles returning from Spain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> helped found the Colegio de Santa Cruz de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> in 1536, the first European school of higher education in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> there in its early years. He also served as a missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlalmanalco">Tlalmanalco</a>, Xochimilco, Tepepulco and Huexotzinco, and translated the Psalms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and a catechism into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>. In 1558 the provincial Francisco de Toral commissioned him to write in Nahuatl on topics he thought useful to the mission.</p><p>At Tepepulco he spent two years interviewing about a dozen village elders, assisted by native graduates of the college who knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Spanish, and who also painted the illustrations. He asked about ritual and calendar, family, economic and political customs, and natural history, and questioned the elders singly and in groups to test their information. This produced the Primeros Memoriales, the basis of the larger Historia general. Between 1561 and 1575 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> and consulted more elders, and he spent his last fifteen or so years editing, translating and copying.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a> of the Historia general has 2,400 pages in twelve books and about 2,500 illustrations by native artists. Its Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> texts face one another, and it records Aztec religion, ritual, society, economy and history. Book 12 tells the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> from the Tenochtitlan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> point of view. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> drew on a range of informants, women among them, and compared their answers, methods that scholars regard as forerunners of modern ethnography.</p><p>Some colonial authorities thought the work dangerous, and the Inquisition was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1570. In 1575 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> banned scriptures in indigenous languages and forced him to hand over his papers, but he made two more copies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a> was catalogued in the Laurentian Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1793, and in 2015 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared his work a World Heritage. He died on 5 February 1590.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernardino de Sahagún” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The surviving codices, and the testimony of indigenous informants compiled by <b>Fray Bernardino de Sahagún</b> in the Florentine Codex, are the main sources for Aztec civilisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>For the Aztecs, Tollan was the model of civilisation: in Nahuatl a toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’, meant art and refinement, while the Nahua informants of <b>Bernardino de Sahagún</b> credited the Toltecs with inventing medicine and the calendar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franciscan <b>Bernardino de Sahagún</b> worked for decades with Nahua scholars to compile the Florentine Codex, completed in the 1570s, a twelve-book encyclopaedia of Aztec gods, rituals, beliefs and society, written in Nahuatl and Spanish and illustrated by Nahua artists; it remains the most important source for Aztec…</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Florentine Codex, completed around 1577 by the Franciscan <b>Bernardino de Sahagún</b> with Nahua scholars from Tlatelolco, is an encyclopaedia of Aztec civilisation in Nahuatl and Spanish, whose twelfth book tells the story of the conquest from the perspective of the Mexica.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Florentine Codex is a 16th-century ethnographic research study in Mesoamerica by the Spanish Franciscan friar <b>Bernardino de Sahagún</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>At their head were the two high priests of the Templo Mayor in Tenochtitlan, one serving Huitzilopochtli and the other Tlaloc, both bearing the title Quetzalcoatl; the friars <b>Bernardino de Sahagún</b> and Diego Durán describe them as respected at every level of society.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.37,-5.03&amp;year=1558" aria-label="Sahagún on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bernardino de Sahagún"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4682 3943h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4682 3943h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.37,-5.03&amp;year=1558">What was here in 1558? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
